  <h2>What this site is becoming</h2>
  <p>Cooked Finance began as a private financial-health experiment. The useful parts were the transparent calculations, local-first privacy, source discipline, and practical next steps. The site now applies those strengths to an everyday financial problem with many recurring decisions: the cost of running and caring for a home.</p>

  <h2>What you can do here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Convert annual electricity or gas use into an annual and average monthly cost.</li>
    <li>Compare a repair quote with an installed replacement price over one planning horizon.</li>
    <li>Learn which exact label metric belongs in the calculation.</li>
    <li>Build a private maintenance plan stored only in your browser.</li>
    <li>Inspect the sources, assumptions, exclusions, and substantive review date for each guide.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we do not claim</h2>
  <p>We do not inspect equipment, diagnose internal faults, predict breakdowns, guarantee savings, or replace product manuals and qualified professionals. Home safety content is intentionally conservative. Smoke, burning odor, shock, sparking, recurring breaker trips, refrigerant work, active leaks near electricity, and pressurized safety devices are outside the DIY scope.</p>

  <h2>U.S.-first scope</h2>
  <p>The initial source set is U.S.-first because it relies on FTC EnergyGuide rules, DOE test procedures, ENERGY STAR product data, EPA guidance, CPSC recalls, and U.S. utility units. The formulas can accept other prices, but regulatory labels, currency, safety resources, and terminology may differ elsewhere.</p>

  <h2>Who publishes the work</h2>
  <p>Cooked Finance is currently an independent publication, not a utility, manufacturer, repair company, government agency, engineering firm, or financial adviser. Until a guide has a genuinely qualified individual reviewer, it is attributed to the publication and does not imply credentials that do not exist.</p>

  <h2>The original money tools</h2>
  <p>The original private financial check and standalone calculators remain available under <a href="/calculators/">Money Tools</a>. They are secondary educational resources and retain their separate <a href="/money-tools/financial-health-check/methodology/">financial model methodology</a>.</p>

